1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,399
This is one of the best-known
images of American

2
00:00:05,399 --> 00:00:08,615
history: Washington Crossing the Delaware.

3
00:00:08,915 --> 00:00:12,164
On a winter river, among
floating ice, George

4
00:00:12,164 --> 00:00:15,783
Washington stands in a small
boat and looks ahead.

5
00:00:16,083 --> 00:00:20,111
Behind him, a soldier raises
the Stars and Stripes.

6
00:00:20,411 --> 00:00:24,727
But this painting was not
made in seventeen seventy-six.

7
00:00:25,027 --> 00:00:28,762
It was made about seventy-five
years later, in Germany.

8
00:00:29,062 --> 00:00:31,919
And on the night
of the crossing, that flag

9
00:00:31,919 --> 00:00:34,981
had not yet been adopted
as the nation's flag.

10
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:39,453
In this episode, we follow
the image through time:

11
00:00:39,453 --> 00:00:43,369
from a winter night on the river,
to a painter's studio

12
00:00:43,369 --> 00:00:47,647
in Düsseldorf, to the city where
the painting became famous.

13
00:00:52,567 --> 00:00:57,402
On the evening of December
twenty-fifth, seventeen seventy-six,

14
00:00:57,402 --> 00:01:01,380
Washington's army began
to cross the Delaware River,

15
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:05,747
from Pennsylvania into New
Jersey, on its way to Trenton.

16
00:01:06,047 --> 00:01:09,333
Two days later, Washington
described the crossing

17
00:01:09,333 --> 00:01:13,030
in a letter to John Hancock,
the president of Congress.

18
00:01:13,933 --> 00:01:16,986
The plan was to start
as soon as it grew dark.

19
00:01:17,286 --> 00:01:21,336
But ice formed in the river
that night, and slowed the boats

20
00:01:21,336 --> 00:01:25,249
so much that it was three
o'clock before all the artillery

21
00:01:25,249 --> 00:01:29,367
was across, and nearly four
before the troops began to march.

22
00:01:29,667 --> 00:01:33,055
He called it a very severe
night, and wrote that they

23
00:01:33,055 --> 00:01:36,182
marched through a violent
storm of snow and hail.

24
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:41,293
In the painting, a morning light
is spreading across the sky,

25
00:01:41,293 --> 00:01:44,299
and a last star still
shines above the boat.

26
00:01:44,599 --> 00:01:48,609
Studies of the painting connect
this light with the coming dawn.

27
00:01:48,909 --> 00:01:53,761
The crossing that went on through the
night is gathered here into a single view.

28
00:01:54,667 --> 00:02:00,339
The flag in the painting has red and white
stripes, and white stars on a blue field.

29
00:02:00,639 --> 00:02:02,847
This is the Stars and Stripes.

30
00:02:03,147 --> 00:02:07,490
On June fourteenth, seventeen
seventy-seven, about six months

31
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:12,268
after the crossing, the Continental
Congress resolved that the flag

32
00:02:12,268 --> 00:02:17,117
of the United States would have
thirteen stripes and thirteen stars.

33
00:02:17,417 --> 00:02:21,419
The resolution did not say how
the stars should be arranged.

34
00:02:21,719 --> 00:02:25,513
Which flag was carried
that night, if any, is not known.

35
00:02:26,433 --> 00:02:31,935
The painter, Emanuel Leutze, was born
in Germany in eighteen sixteen.

36
00:02:32,235 --> 00:02:36,466
As a child, he moved with his family
to the United States.

37
00:02:36,766 --> 00:02:41,653
In eighteen forty-one, he went
back to Germany to study painting,

38
00:02:41,653 --> 00:02:45,853
in Düsseldorf, a city known
for its painters of history.

39
00:02:46,767 --> 00:02:51,736
He began the first version of this
picture there in eighteen forty-nine,

40
00:02:51,736 --> 00:02:56,355
soon after revolutions swept across
Europe in eighteen forty-eight.

41
00:02:56,655 --> 00:03:00,306
Mount Vernon, Washington's
home and now a museum,

42
00:03:00,306 --> 00:03:04,719
explains that Leutze supported
those revolutions, and hoped

43
00:03:04,719 --> 00:03:09,740
his painting would inspire new
struggles for liberty and democracy.

44
00:03:10,667 --> 00:03:13,825
Americans in the city
posed for the figures.

45
00:03:14,125 --> 00:03:18,413
One of them, the painter Worthington
Whittredge, recalled that he

46
00:03:18,413 --> 00:03:23,037
posed for the man steering the boat,
and again for Washington himself.

47
00:03:23,337 --> 00:03:28,455
For Washington's head, Leutze is said
to have looked to a sculpture by Houdon.

48
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:34,709
In November eighteen fifty, a fire
in the studio damaged the first version.

49
00:03:35,009 --> 00:03:38,027
The next year, Leutze began a second one.

50
00:03:38,327 --> 00:03:41,881
The first was repaired,
went to the city of Bremen,

51
00:03:41,881 --> 00:03:45,363
and was lost in an air
raid in nineteen forty-two.

52
00:03:46,267 --> 00:03:49,209
In eighteen fifty-one, the second version

53
00:03:49,209 --> 00:03:52,655
was shown in New York,
and crowds came to see it.

54
00:03:52,955 --> 00:03:57,267
Two years later, an engraving
after the painting was published,

55
00:03:57,267 --> 00:04:00,675
carrying the image far
beyond the exhibition room.

56
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:04,380
In eighteen ninety-seven, it was given

57
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:08,403
to the Metropolitan Museum
of Art, where it hangs today.

58
00:04:08,703 --> 00:04:11,042
It is more than six meters wide.

59
00:04:11,967 --> 00:04:15,691
So, did Washington really
cross the Delaware like this?

60
00:04:15,991 --> 00:04:18,908
The painting was made
about seventy-five years

61
00:04:18,908 --> 00:04:21,761
after the night it shows,
in another country.

62
00:04:22,061 --> 00:04:25,272
Its painter put the light
of morning in the sky,

63
00:04:25,272 --> 00:04:28,757
and the later flag of a nation
in a soldier's hands.

64
00:04:29,057 --> 00:04:31,232
It is not a record of that night.

65
00:04:31,532 --> 00:04:35,626
It is a picture of that night,
made for the painter's own time.

66
00:04:35,926 --> 00:04:39,629
And today, it is the picture
that many of us remember.

67
00:04:40,533 --> 00:04:42,861
Every story has an origin.

68
00:04:43,161 --> 00:04:48,448
Sometimes, the image we remember
was painted long after the moment itself.

